{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need count binary strings length 12 that avoid forbidden substrings \"111\" and \"000\" as contiguous. So no run of 3 same bits. Count sequences of length 12 over {0,1} with no 000 or 111. This is classic counting using recurrence. Equivalent to number of binary strings of length n with no 3 consecutive equal bits. Let a_n be number of strings length n ending with run of length 1 of some bit? We can use DP with states: last two bits? Actually we need to track last two bits to avoid creating 3 same. Let's define states: ending with ...0 (last bit 0) and previous bit maybe 0 or 1. But we need to know run length of last bit: 1 or 2. So states: (bit, run length). So 4 states: (0,1), (0,2), (1,1), (1,2). Transition: from (0,1) we can add 0 -> run length 2 => (0,2). Add 1 -> run length 1 => (1,1). Similarly (0,2) cannot add 0 (would make 3). So only add 1 -> (1,1). Similarly for 1 states. So we can compute counts.\n\nLet dp[n][b][r] counts length n ending with bit b run length r. Base n=1: dp[1][0][1]=1, dp[1][1][1]=1. Others 0.\n\nRecurrence for n>1:\ndp[n][0][1] = dp[n-1][1][1] + dp[n-1][1][2] (adding 0 after a 1 run)\ndp[n][0][2] = dp[n-1][0][1] (adding 0 after run length 1)\ndp[n][1][1] = dp[n-1][0][1] + dp[n-1][0][2]\ndp[n][1][2] = dp[n-1][1][1]\n\nWe can compute up to n=12. Let's do quickly.\n\nInitialize arrays.\n\nn=1:\n(0,1)=1\n(0,2)=0\n(1,1)=1\n(1,2)=0\n\nn=2:\n(0,1)= dp1[1][1]+dp1[1][2] =1+0=1\n(0,2)= dp1[0][1]=1\n(1,1)= dp1[0][1]+dp1[0][2] =1+0=1\n(1,2)= dp1[1][1]=1\n\nCheck totals: 1+1+1+1=4. All 4 strings length2: 00,01,10,11. Good.\n\nn=3:\n(0,1)= dp2[1][1]+dp2[1][2] =1+1=2\n(0,2)= dp2[0][1]=1\n(1,1)= dp2[0][1]+dp2[0][2] =1+1=2\n(1,2)= dp2[1][1]=1\n\nTotals: 2+1+2+1=6. Strings length3 avoiding 000 and 111: indeed 6.\n\nn=4:\n(0,1)= dp3[1][1]+dp3[1][2] =2+1=3\n(0,2)= dp3[0][1]=2\n(1,1)= dp3[0"}}],"created":1790936616,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Do